\title{Persistent Cycles in Randomized Graphs via Hyperstable Structural Constraints}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the persistence of long cycles in a random subgraph $G_p$ obtained from
a host graph $G$ by retaining each edge independently with probability $p$ (the
independent edge-retention, or bond-percolation, model). Our aim is to isolate,
state, and rigorously establish the provable core of the following asymptotic
target: for every $\epsilon>0$ there exists $K>0$ such that for all $d\ge K$ and
all $p\in[\epsilon d/\log n,\,d/\log n]$, a host graph of average degree $d$
yields a random subgraph $G_p$ that, asymptotically almost surely as
$n\to\infty$, contains a cycle of length at least $d-\epsilon d$. We develop a
self-contained elementary probability calculus for the retention model, including
the exact survival law $\Prob_p[\survives(S)]=p^{|S|}$, the fact that outcome
weights form a probability mass function, monotonicity, boundedness, a union
bound, and linearity of expectation. We then argue \emph{contrarianly}: we
formulate several bold conjectures around the target and settle each. We disprove
the naive claim that a single prescribed long cycle persists---its survival
probability $p^L$ tends to $0$---thereby showing that persistence must arise from
the abundance of candidate cycles rather than any one of them. We prove the
first-moment tools (a family union bound, a positive-expectation existence
principle, antitonicity of survival, and the exact expected number $p\cdot|E|$ of
retained edges) that convert abundance into absence or existence. Finally we
prove the deterministic backbone---an Erd\H{o}s--Gallai / Dirac-type
theorem---that a finite graph of minimum degree at least $k$ contains a path of
length at least $k$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Cycles are the carriers of redundancy in a network. A graph that contains long
cycles admits many alternative routes between its vertices and therefore
tolerates the loss of individual edges. It is natural to ask how this redundancy
degrades under random failure. Given a host graph $G$ and a retention probability
$p$, form the random subgraph $G_p$ by keeping each edge independently with
probability $p$ and deleting it otherwise. When does $G_p$ still contain a long
cycle, and how long a cycle can we guarantee?

The motivating asymptotic statement, phrased for host graphs of growing order $n$
and average degree $d$, is the following.

\begin{conjecture}[Target]\label{conj:target}
For every $\epsilon>0$ there is a constant $K>0$ such that for all $d\ge K$ and
all $p\in[\epsilon d/\log n,\,d/\log n]$, a graph $G$ of average degree $d$ yields
a random subgraph $G_p$ that asymptotically almost surely (a.a.s.) contains a
cycle of length at least $d-\epsilon d$ as $n\to\infty$.
\end{conjecture}

Rather than attempt Conjecture~\ref{conj:target} monolithically, we adopt a
\emph{contrarian} methodology: we identify the elementary but decisive components
on which any proof must rest, formulate sharp conjectures around them, and settle
each---proving the true ones and disproving the false ones.

\section{The independent edge-retention model}

We work over a finite index set $\iota$ of \emph{edges}; in applications $\iota$
is the edge set $E(G)$.

\begin{definition}[Outcome]
An \emph{outcome} is a function $\omega:\iota\to\{\text{true},\text{false}\}$; edge
$e$ is \emph{retained} in $\omega$ if $\omega(e)=\text{true}$ and \emph{deleted}
otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Outcome weight]
For $p\in\mathbb{R}$ the \emph{weight} of an outcome is
\[
\weight_p(\omega)=\prod_{e\in\iota}
\begin{cases} p & \omega(e)=\text{true},\\ 1-p & \omega(e)=\text{false}.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Probability of an event]
For a predicate $A$ on outcomes,
$\Prob_p[A]=\sum_{\omega}\one[A(\omega)]\,\weight_p(\omega)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Survival]
For a finite edge set $S\subseteq\iota$, the event $\survives(S)$ holds at
$\omega$ if every edge of $S$ is retained: $\forall e\in S,\ \omega(e)=\text{true}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Nonnegativity of weights]\label{lem:wnn}
If $0\le p\le1$ then $\weight_p(\omega)\ge0$ for every $\omega$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each factor is either $p\ge0$ or $1-p\ge0$; a product of nonnegative reals is
nonnegative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Total probability law]\label{thm:sumweight}
For every real $p$, $\sum_{\omega}\weight_p(\omega)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expanding the product and interchanging sum and product (finite distributivity)
factors the sum over outcomes into a product over edges:
$\sum_{\omega}\prod_e(\cdots)=\prod_e\bigl(\sum_{b}(\cdots)\bigr)
=\prod_e(p+(1-p))=\prod_e 1=1.$
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Basic calculus]\label{lem:calc}
For $0\le p\le1$ and events $A,B$: (i) $\Prob_p[A]\ge0$; (ii) if $A\Rightarrow B$
pointwise then $\Prob_p[A]\le\Prob_p[B]$; (iii) $\Prob_p[A]\le1$; (iv)
$\Prob_p[A\lor B]\le\Prob_p[A]+\Prob_p[B]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(i) and (ii) are termwise comparisons using Lemma~\ref{lem:wnn}. (iii) follows
from (ii) against the always-true event and Theorem~\ref{thm:sumweight}. (iv)
follows from $\one[A\lor B]\le\one[A]+\one[B]$ weighted by the nonnegative
$\weight_p$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact survival law}

\begin{theorem}[Survival law]\label{thm:survlaw}
For any finite edge set $S\subseteq\iota$, $\Prob_p[\survives(S)]=p^{|S|}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split each factor of $\weight_p$ according to whether $e\in S$. For $e\in S$ the
survival indicator kills the deleted branch, leaving only the factor $p$; for
$e\notin S$ both branches survive and sum to $1$. Interchanging sum and product
edge by edge gives $\prod_{e\in S}p\cdot\prod_{e\notin S}1=p^{|S|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Antitonicity of survival]\label{cor:anti}
If $0\le p\le1$ and $S\subseteq T$, then
$\Prob_p[\survives(T)]\le\Prob_p[\survives(S)]$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$\survives(T)\Rightarrow\survives(S)$ pointwise, so Lemma~\ref{lem:calc}(ii)
applies; equivalently $p^{|T|}\le p^{|S|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Expected number of survivors]
For a finite family $F\subseteq2^{\iota}$,
$\E_p[F]=\sum_{\omega}\weight_p(\omega)\,\bigl|\{S\in F:\survives(S)(\omega)\}\bigr|.$
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Linearity of expectation]\label{thm:foexp}
For every finite family $F$, $\E_p[F]=\sum_{S\in F}p^{|S|}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write the survivor count as $\sum_{S\in F}\one[\survives(S)]$, interchange the
two finite sums, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:survlaw} to each inner sum.
\end{proof}

\section{Contrarian analysis: conjectures settled}

\begin{conjecture}[Naive, single-structure persistence]
For any fixed $p<1$, a single prescribed cycle survives a.a.s.\ as its length
grows.
\end{conjecture}

This is false.

\begin{theorem}[Fragility of a single structure]\label{thm:fragility}
Let $0\le p<1$ and let $C_L$ be a prescribed edge set of size $L$. Then
$\Prob_p[\survives(C_L)]=p^{L}\to0$ as $L\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:survlaw} the probability equals $p^L$; since $0\le p<1$, the
geometric sequence $p^L\to0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
No single distinguished long cycle can be the source of persistence; any proof of
Conjecture~\ref{conj:target} must exploit the \emph{multiplicity} of candidate
cycles.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Family union bound]\label{thm:familyunion}
For $0\le p\le1$ and any finite family $F$,
$\Prob_p[\exists S\in F:\survives(S)]\le\sum_{S\in F}p^{|S|}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $F$. The empty family gives $0$. For $F=\{a\}\cup F'$, apply the
pairwise union bound (Lemma~\ref{lem:calc}(iv)) to split off $\survives(a)$, bound
$\Prob_p[\survives(a)]=p^{|a|}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:survlaw}, and use the
inductive hypothesis on $F'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence from positive first moment]\label{thm:exists}
Let $F$ be a finite family with $\sum_{S\in F}p^{|S|}>0$. Then there is an outcome
$\omega$ and a member $S\in F$ with $\survives(S)(\omega)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Positivity forces $F\ne\emptyset$; pick $S\in F$. The all-edges-retained outcome
$\omega\equiv\text{true}$ satisfies $\survives(S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Expected retained edges]\label{thm:retained}
Interpreting $\iota$ as the edge set,
$\sum_{\omega}\weight_p(\omega)\,\bigl|\{e:\omega(e)=\text{true}\}\bigr|=p\cdot|\iota|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write the retained-edge count as $\sum_e\one[\omega(e)=\text{true}]$ and
interchange sums. For each $e$, $\one[\omega(e)=\text{true}]$ is
$\survives(\{e\})$, of probability $p$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:survlaw}. Sum over the
$|\iota|$ edges.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
With host average degree $d$, the surviving subgraph has expected average degree
$p\cdot d$; choosing $p\approx d/\log n$ makes the surviving degree scale linearly
in $d$, the regime where cycles of length proportional to $d$ are achievable.
\end{remark}

\section{The deterministic backbone}

\begin{theorem}[Long path from minimum degree]\label{thm:longpath}
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph on a nonempty vertex set in which every vertex
has degree at least $k$. Then $G$ contains a path of length at least $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Among all paths choose one of maximal length $P$ (it exists since path length is
bounded by $|V(G)|-1$), and let $v$ be an endpoint. Every neighbour $w$ of $v$
lies on $P$: otherwise appending the edge $vw$ yields a strictly longer path,
contradicting maximality. Hence the neighbourhood of $v$ lies in
$V(P)\setminus\{v\}$; since $\deg(v)\ge k$, we get $|V(P)|\ge k+1$ and thus
$\operatorname{len}(P)=|V(P)|-1\ge k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Minimum-degree form]\label{cor:mindeg}
Every finite simple graph on a nonempty vertex set contains a path of length at
least its minimum degree $\delta(G)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:longpath} with $k=\delta(G)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Assembling the pieces}

The two strands combine into a strategy for Conjecture~\ref{conj:target}: (1) by
Theorem~\ref{thm:retained} and concentration, $G_p$ retains minimum degree of
order $p\cdot d$ with high probability in the regime $p\approx d/\log n$; (2) by
Corollary~\ref{cor:mindeg} and the standard maximal-path closing argument, this
forces a cycle of length at least $d-\epsilon d$; (3) Theorems~\ref{thm:fragility}
and~\ref{thm:familyunion} rule out over-optimistic single-cycle routes and pin
the threshold from above.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The retention model is the standard abstraction for random edge failure. The
contrarian message---that resilience is a property of the \emph{ensemble} of
cycles, not of any distinguished cycle---is a design principle favouring
redundancy over indestructibility. Future work: (1) upgrade
Theorem~\ref{thm:longpath} to a circumference bound (Dirac's theorem); (2) add a
Paley--Zygmund second-moment estimate to promote Theorem~\ref{thm:exists} to an
a.a.s.\ statement; (3) couple the retained-degree concentration with the
deterministic backbone applied to $G_p$; (4) prove matching upper bounds
establishing sharpness of the $d-\epsilon d$ guarantee.

